kaccayana.github.io - Licence
=============================

The original work in this repository - the English translations of Kaccāyana,
the Padarūpasiddhi and Bālāvatāra, the commentary and notes, the diagrams, the site code and its
configuration - is dedicated to the public domain under the Creative Commons
CC0 1.0 Universal dedication, whose legal code follows this notice.

The material listed below is reproduced from other sources. It is NOT covered
by the CC0 dedication: each item stays under the terms stated for it, and
nothing in this file grants any right in it beyond what its rights holder
allows.

1. Pāli source texts

   The Pāli text of Kaccāyana, of Buddhappiya's Padarūpasiddhi and of Bālāvatāra
   quoted throughout the site, and the complete Bālāvatāra text in
   src/content/docs/balavatara/cscd4.md,
   come from the Chaṭṭha Saṅgāyana Tipiṭaka edition published by the
   Vipassana Research Institute at https://tipitaka.org. The Institute
   publishes these texts without a stated licence and asks that its site be
   cited; they are reproduced here with that attribution.

2. Bālāvatāra, 1935 University of Calcutta translation

   "Bālāvatāra: An Elementary Pali Grammar Abridged for the Under-graduate
   Course", originally edited and translated by Satischandra Vidyabhusana and
   Punnananda Swami, revised and recast by Sailendranath Mitra, University of
   Calcutta, 1935. Transcribed in src/content/docs/balavatara/mitra.md; a scan
   of the printed book is served as public/reference/balavatara-1935-mitra.pdf.
   Reproduced as a historical text; any copyright that still subsists remains
   with its holders.

3. U Nandisena, Kaccāyanabyākaraṇaṃ (2005, 2017)

   "Kaccāyanabyākaraṇaṃ", translated into English by U Nandisena. First
   published in Myanmar in 2005; first electronic version 2017, Copyright
   © 2017 Instituto de Estudios Buddhistas Hispano (IEBH), publication
   IEBH: 20170116-BN-T0100, admin@iebh.org. Licensed by IEBH under the
   Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-NoDerivatives 4.0 International
   licence (CC BY-NC-ND 4.0), https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/.
   Reproduced as the page src/content/docs/kaccayana/nandisena.md and as the
   PDF files public/reference/nandisena-2005-kaccayanabyakaranam-text.pdf (the
   translator's 2005 text) and
   public/reference/nandisena-2017-kaccayanabyakaranam-scan.pdf (the 2017
   edition). Use of this material must follow that licence: credit the
   translator and IEBH, no commercial use, no derivative works.

4. Phramaha Thiab Malai, Kaccāyana-Vyākaraṇa: A Critical Study (1997)

   Ph.D. thesis, University of Pune, 1997, two volumes, reproduced from the
   scans on archive.org as a cleaned reading edition in src/content/docs/malai/
   (typing errors corrected and listed on its Corrections page). Copyright
   remains with the author; no licence is granted here.

5. Articles and reviews by Aleix Ruiz-Falqués

   Reproduced for study as pages in src/content/docs/reference/ (with their
   figures) and as PDF files in public/reference/. Copyright in each remains
   with the author and the publisher named; no licence is granted here.

   - "On the Authorship of Kaccāyana, the Oldest Pāli Grammar", in
     Bimalendra Kumar and Ujjwal Kumar (eds.), Pariyatti: Studies in Pāli
     Language and Literature, New Delhi: Aditya Prakashan, 2017, pp. 251-268.
     Copyright © 2017 Editors.
   - "The Role of Pāli Grammar in Burmese Buddhism", Journal of Burma Studies
     21.1 (2017), pp. 1-96, Northern Illinois University Press,
     https://doi.org/10.1353/jbs.2017.0001. © 2017 Center for Burma Studies,
     Northern Illinois University. PDF only.
   - Review of Ole Holten Pind (ed.), Kaccāyana and Kaccāyanavutti,
     Orientalistische Literaturzeitung 113.4-5 (2018), pp. 392-395, Walter de
     Gruyter, https://doi.org/10.1515/olzg-2018-0129.
   - Review of Ashin U Thitzana, Kaccāyana Pāli Grammar (2016), Journal of
     Ñāṇasaṃvara Centre for Buddhist Studies I (2018), pp. 279-303. © 2018
     Ñāṇasaṃvara Centre for Buddhist Studies, the Foundation of His Holiness
     Somdet Phra Ñāṇasaṃvara Centennial Commemoration under the Patronage of
     His Holiness the Supreme Patriarch.
   - "Boundaries and Domains: Understanding Optionality in Buddhappiya's
     Rūpasiddhi", Journal of the Pali Text Society XXXIV (2021), pp. 227-250.
     Pali Text Society.
   - "The Sword and the Sheath. Three Notes on Kaccāyana 1: Attho
     Akkharasaññāto", in Bimalendra Kumar and Ujjwal Kumar (eds.),
     Dhamma-Anusīlana: Investigating Papers on Buddhist Studies, New Delhi:
     Aditya Prakashan, 2021, pp. 373-400.
   - "Two levels of optionality in the Kaccāyana Vyākaraṇa", in Peter M.
     Scharf (ed.), Śabdānugamaḥ: Indian linguistic studies in honor of George
     Cardona, vol. I, Providence: The Sanskrit Library, 2021, pp. 431-465.
     Copyright © 2021 by The Sanskrit Library, all rights reserved.
   - "Silent Mastery: The Concealed Art of Buddhist Writing", Numen 71 (2024),
     pp. 303-314, https://doi.org/10.1163/15685276-20240007. © Aleix
     Ruiz-Falqués 2024, published with licence by Koninklijke Brill NV.
     PDF only.

